#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133536 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133933 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af17da8-272b-55fb-8a5d-5857bd41051e" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula Using Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "5537647b-d722-5000-8445-b2743cc1f1f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-1512" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n) =\\dfrac{6}{(-2)^n}$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = r \\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1$. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= (6)/((-2)^{1}) = (6)/((-2)) =-3, \\\\\nf(2) &= (6)/((-2)^{2}) = \\frac{6}{4} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = (f(2))/(f(1)) = ((\\frac{3}{2}))/((-3)) =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = -\\dfrac{1}{2} f(n), \\qquad f(1) = -3, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac7a8723-02b7-5467-98f7-530ba8239c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "19d183f9-cf05-5477-8da9-642e2fd8e03b" :question/math-academy-id "e-1513" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence\n$a_{n+1} = 3a_n,\\, \\qquad a_1 = 1,\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $a_1$ and the common ratio $r$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1=1$. To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we see that $r = 3$.\n\nSubstituting $a_1 = 1$ and $r = 3$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &= a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 1 \\cdot 3^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 3^{n-1}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=3^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133550 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133932 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f258f110-e541-5edf-8050-54d03177e499" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "6aa991c4-28e3-51fc-9642-19fa9346202d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4237" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $a_n = 8 \\cdot 3^n$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1$. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 8 \\cdot 3^{1} = 8 \\cdot 3 = 24, \\\\\na_{2} &= 8 \\cdot 3^{2} = 8 \\cdot 9 = 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{72}{24} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = 3 a_n, \\quad a_1 = 24, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a910df9-7b4a-5892-a084-52d8b5dcd086" :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula Using Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "5027a265-fd62-574b-b6fa-2bacf9de7304" :question/math-academy-id "e-4238" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the sequence\n$f(n+1) =-\\dfrac{f(n)}{3},\\, \\quad f(1) = {\\color{blue}9},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1$?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n + 1) = - (f(n))/(3) =-\\frac{1}{3}f(n)\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n) =f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is\n\n$$\nf(1)=9\n$$\n\nTo find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = {\\color{red}r} \\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we see that\n\n$$\nr = -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nf(1) = 9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nr = -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(n) &= f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 9 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{n-1}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(n) &= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{-1} \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n} \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{1}) \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n} \\\\\n&=-27 \\cdot (1)/((-3)^{n}) \\\\\n&= - (27)/((-3)^{n})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=-\\dfrac{27}{(-3)^n},\\quad n\\geq 1\n$$"}]}
